('practice:chemistry:p1:q1909:en:v1','chemistry',1909,'Atomic structure','Deflection in an electric field',
 'answer_explanation','en','iclub_practice_ai_chemistry_p1_atomic_structure_precise_v1_2026_09_25','Deflection in an electric field','In an electric field, a charged particle experiences force F = qE. For particles with the same speed and equal charge magnitude, the lighter particle has the greater acceleration and is deflected more. Electrons and protons have equal charge magnitude, but the electron has far smaller mass. A neutron has zero charge, so an electric field does not deflect it.',
